          DIGITAL BREAKS NETWORK MANAGEMENT COST BARRIERS, 
             ADDS TEAM COLLABORATION TO THE INTERNET


NetWorld + Interop, ATLANTA -- September 25, 1995 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced at NetWorld + Interop 95 Atlanta five new 
software products that simplify and lower the cost of network access, 
collaboration and management.  

     Digital's new network management products for Windows NT --
POLYCENTER Manager on NetView and ManageWORKS -- offer important ease
of use enhancements and a degree of price/performance unmatched in the
industry. The Workgroup Web products announced today give Internet
users "out-of-the-box" team collaboration capabilities. The latest
release of the PATHWORKS PC LAN integration software meets the demands
of 21st century computing for transparent access, sharing and
administration of information worldwide.

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT

     With the introduction of POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows
NT, Digital slashes the cost of installing an enterprise management
solution by two-thirds, and opens a clear path for network managers
employing Windows-based tools to easily handle the increasing
management requirements of rapidly-growing networks. 

     POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT V3.0 is a port of the
IBM AIX- and Digital UNIX-based NetView product. Combining the
award-winning NetView Motif graphical user interface with an
implementation of the Windows 95 user interface style, POLYCENTER
Manager on NetView creates a more intuitive interface which
significantly reduces training costs. Some of the Windows 95 features
included with POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT are wizards,
property sheets, movable tool pallets and stackable map windows. In
addition, its tight bi-directional integration with Microsoft's System
Management Server and POLYCENTER AssetWORKS creates what industry
analyst Larry Abbott, senior editor of "Managing Distributed Systems"
from Faulkner Information Services calls a "...killer network/system
management suite on NT." 
    
     POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT, available on Sept.
28, is priced at $6,995 and runs on a minimum configuration of an Intel
80486 system with 24 MB memory and 100 MB hard disk. A version for
Digital's Alpha technology will be available by the end of 1995,
providing an even more powerful upgrade path for network managers.

     Digital also announced that over 40 management application vendors
have committed to support POLYCENTER NetView on Windows NT. 
Applications available today include StoneyBrook's RouterManager
Autobahn, a management solution for multivendor WAN backbone routers. 
Digital is also developing several other applications for Windows NT,
including POLYCENTER DECnet Manager, for the Digital network
environment, and ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator, which integrates
with POLYCENTER Manager on NetView to provide comprehensive multi-NOS
management. 

Workgroup Web and Workgroup Web Forum

     With the announcement of the Workgroup Web and Workgroup Web Forum
products, Digital combines the World Wide Web (WWW) paradigm with team
collaboration capabilities. The two applications add out-of-the-box
team collaboration to graphical web browsers such as Netscape and
Mosaic, providing point-and-click hyperlinks to corporate information
in LANs, WANs and across the Internet. Other features include online
conferencing, keyword searching and polling capabilities. 

     Workgroup Web is the easy, fun way to share information online and
provides a perfect starting point to the Internet. Working over any
network operating system, Workgroup Web takes advantage of existing
file and print services, incorporating their security attributes, and
lets small teams up to 25 members organize information by projects,
activities, information type, people or other categories. With or
without an actual Internet connection, the Workgroup Web shows users
how Internet technologies can improve existing business processes
through the easy creation of informal, ad hoc teams requiring minimal
training. Workgroup Web is priced at $99 and runs on any Intel 80386SX
(or greater) system with 8 MB RAM. 

     Workgroup Web Forum extends the capabilities of an Internet
Worldwide Web server, using Internet or TCP/IP LAN connections to
provide unique conferencing capabilities not available with existing
web servers. By enabling team members and web site visitors to
communicate interactively around the world through private or public
connections, it greatly expands business opportunities.

     Workgroup Web Forum works with all graphics browsers on both
private corporate internets and on the Internet to enhance conferencing 
capabilities and offer real-time polling. It takes advantage or
additional security capabilities like firewalls, gateways and
encryption software. Priced at $1,995, Workgroup Web Forum V1.0 will
be available at the end of October and runs on any Alpha or Intel
server platform capable of supporting Digital UNIX or Windows NT with
the appropriate HTTP server. 

PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows V6.0

     The introduction of V6.0 of PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows is a
major step forward in removing the barriers from finding, accessing and
managing resources on expanding corporate networks. Employing the new
directory assistant feature, PATHWORKS V6.0 creates easy-to-understand
terms for network printers and files (such as 'Finance's Color
PostScript Printer',) facilitates information sharing among workgroups
and simplifies system administration.

     PATHWORKS V6.0 includes the new Workgroup Web application, adding
value to existing NetWare, Windows NT and LAN Manager-based network
operating systems. Employing protected mode protocols, PATHWORKS V6.0
frees conventional memory for use by applications. It also integrates
Windows 95 clients today, providing unlimited access to OpenVMS and
Digital UNIX PATHWORKS servers. For simplified network administration,
PATHWORKS V6.0 automatically gathers information on printers and shared
directories, enabling managers to build a more reliable and flexible
network environment. PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows V6.0 runs on any
Intel-based PC and is priced at $205. The upgrade price for existing
PATHWORKS users is $99. PATHWORKS V6.0 will ship in mid-November. 

ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator V2.2

     Digital also announced today a new version of the industry's first
multi-NOS and SNMP management application, ManageWORKS Workgroup
Administrator. Version 2.2 now runs on Windows 95 and Windows NT
platforms (both Alpha and Intel) and includes several enhancements to
simplify administration of NetWare, LAN Manager, IBM and PATHWORKS
network operating systems.

     A significant enhancement is the new Object Creator facility, which
allows integration of hundreds of DOS or Windows management
applications with ManageWORKS for any aspect of PC LAN administration. 
ManageWORKS also integrates with enterprise management products such as
POLYCENTER Manager on NetView, enabling it to perform as a mid-level
manager for workgroups or remote sites.
    
     Another enhancement to ManageWORKS V2.2 is the inclusion of a
TCP/IP protocol stack for use with Windows 3.x and Workgroup for
Windows desktops. ManageWORKS also supports third party TCP/IP stacks,
including the Microsoft stack provided with Windows 95 and Windows NT. 
ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator V2.2 is priced at $995 and will
ship in mid-October. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, storage,
networking, software and services, together with industry-focused
solutions from business partners, help organizations compete and win in
today's global marketplace.
